<Layout title="Understand ontologies" description="A plain-language guide to what an ontology is, why SPDTF uses one, and what ontology modelling can and cannot establish.">
  <h1>What an ontology is and why SPDTF uses one</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    An ontology is an agreed model of the things a community cares about: what they are,
    how they are identified, what words mean, and how those things relate. SPDTF uses an
    ontology so meaning can survive a change of form, file, system or organisation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what">What an ontology is</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine a shared map rather than a large spreadsheet. The map names important kinds
    of thing—such as a property, legal title, address, seller, lender or inspection—and
    gives their connections explicit names. Each important meaning gets a stable
    identifier, so two systems can refer to the same concept without depending on the
    same column heading or JSON path.
  </p>
  <p>
    The ontology is not only a diagram. Its definitions and relationships are encoded as
    a graph that software can query, compare and validate. Human-readable definitions,
    examples and review questions remain essential because the graph is only useful when
    practitioners agree that it reflects their work.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>How an ontology differs from related artefacts</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Artefact</th><th scope="col">Primary question</th><th scope="col">Relationship to the ontology</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Business glossary</th><td>What does this term mean to practitioners?</td><td>Supplies labels, definitions, examples and ownership.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Data dictionary</th><td>What data is recorded and with what expectations?</td><td>Connects data elements to the things and meanings they describe.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Taxonomy</th><td>How are concepts organised more broadly or narrowly?</td><td>One governed structure within the wider semantic package.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">JSON Schema</th><td>What is the allowed structure of this JSON exchange?</td><td>A possible tested delivery projection when generated; it does not own the meaning.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Database schema</th><td>How does one application store and constrain its records?</td><td>An implementation design that can map to ontology meanings.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="tree-graph">A document tree and a meaning graph</h2>
  <p>
    A document tree is excellent for sending one package: every value has a predictable
    place. The difficulty appears when the same property, address or evidence is needed
    in several messages. A graph identifies it once and lets many records connect to it.
  </p>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="A document tree fixes values beneath one message. A meaning graph identifies reusable things and connects several records to them without copying their identity into every branch.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Document tree compared with a connected meaning graph
  accDescr: The document side nests an address and title under one transaction, while the graph side connects a transaction, property and evidence to reusable identified resources.
  Message["Transaction document"]:::data
  TreeAddress["address field"]:::data
  TreeTitle["title field"]:::data
  Transaction["Transaction"]:::process
  Property["Property"]:::service
  Address["Identified address"]:::infra
  Title["Legal title"]:::security
  Evidence["Source evidence"]:::external
  Message --> TreeAddress
  Message --> TreeTitle
  Transaction --> Property
  Property --> Address
  Property --> Title
  Evidence --> Property
  </Diagram>
  <p>
    This does not make the graph better for every task. An API consumer may still need a
    compact JSON tree. The tree can be generated from, and traced to, a meaning model that
    is not trapped inside that one representation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why SPDTF needs connected meaning</h2>
  <p>
    A property transaction crosses estate agency, conveyancing, finance, surveying,
    data services and technology. Those professions sometimes use the same word for
    different things and different words for the same thing. A single universal list
    would either erase useful distinctions or force one profession’s meaning on another.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Shared meaning:</strong> definitions and identifiers are independent of one supplier’s field names.</li>
    <li><strong>Local precision:</strong> bounded contexts retain distinctions that matter to their work.</li>
    <li><strong>Safe exchange:</strong> a small common boundary carries genuinely shared elements, while <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">qualified mappings</a> relate distinct context-owned concepts after review.</li>
    <li><strong>Traceability:</strong> a candidate can link back to evidence, questions, review and disposition.</li>
    <li><strong>Reusable delivery:</strong> the same agreement can support schemas, APIs, forms and documentation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="worked-example">A small property example</h2>
  <p>
    Read the graph below as sentences: a property <em>has address</em> an identified
    address; a legal title <em>is registered over</em> the property; and a source record
    <em>supports</em> a separate assertion about that title. The edge names carry meaning.
  </p>
  <Diagram size="medium" caption="A six-node example of identifiable things, a claim and named relationships. Literal fields such as an address line stay as values on the relevant resource rather than becoming free-standing topology nodes.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: A property connected to address title claim and evidence
  accDescr: A property links to an identified address and legal title; a source record supports a separate claim about the title and a transaction concerns the property.
  Transaction["Transaction"]:::process
  Property["Property"]:::service
  Address["Address"]:::infra
  Title["Legal title"]:::security
  Claim["Title assertion"]:::data
  Source["Source record"]:::external
  Transaction -->|concerns| Property
  Property -->|has address| Address
  Title -->|registered over| Property
  Claim -->|describes| Title
  Source -->|supports| Claim
  </Diagram>

  <h2 id="limits">What an ontology cannot establish</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It cannot prove that a source statement is true, current or trustworthy.</li>
    <li>It cannot decide which organisation is legally authoritative for a fact.</li>
    <li>It cannot approve a candidate standard or grant OPDA statutory authority.</li>
    <li>It cannot implement consent, authentication or runtime access control.</li>
    <li>It cannot guarantee that every generated schema, form or API preserves meaning.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Automated checks establish only what their declared rules test. Working groups judge
    business meaning; recorded governance controls promotion. The current Property Pack
    ontology remains machine-proposed even where every technical check passes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="participate">What nontechnical participants review</h2>
  <p>
    Participants review definitions, examples, counterexamples, source evidence,
    questions, relationships and proposed rules in readable views. Facilitators maintain
    the technical representation. If a diagram or triple contradicts the business
    explanation, the business disagreement must be resolved rather than hidden in syntax.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/reading-the-model">Next: learn how to read the model →</a></p>
</Layout>
